1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY
Logistics and supply chain management are essential components of modern business operations. Organizations must ensure that products are available at the right location, at the right time, and at an acceptable operational cost. However, businesses frequently experience challenges such as inaccurate demand forecasting, excess inventory, stock shortages, high warehouse expenses, and inefficient resource allocation.
This project proposes a data-driven inventory management and demand forecasting framework for a retail distribution business. The primary objective is to investigate how historical retail transaction data and Python-based data science techniques can improve inventory planning and operational efficiency.
The project uses the publicly available Online Retail II dataset from the UCI Machine Learning Repository. The dataset contains historical retail transaction information, including product codes, quantities, transaction dates, prices, and customer details.
Python libraries such as Pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib, Seaborn, and Scikit-learn will support data cleaning, exploratory data analysis, predictive modeling, and product segmentation.
The proposed analytical techniques include regression analysis, time-series forecasting, K-Means clustering, and inventory optimization.
Five major Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) will be considered: demand forecast accuracy, inventory turnover ratio, stockout rate, order fulfillment rate, and inventory holding cost.
The expected outcome is a practical analytical framework that can support more accurate purchasing decisions, improve inventory allocation, reduce unnecessary storage costs, and strengthen supply chain decision-making.
The project also demonstrates the importance of converting historical business data into meaningful operational insights through structured data analysis.
________________________________________
2. INTRODUCTION AND BACKGROUND RESEARCH
2.1 Background of the Project
Logistics management involves planning, implementing, and controlling the movement and storage of goods, services, and related information from the point of origin to the point of consumption.
Inventory management is one of the most important activities within logistics because it directly influences operational expenses, customer satisfaction, and business profitability.
Maintaining excessive inventory can increase storage expenses, working capital requirements, insurance costs, and the risk of obsolete products.
Conversely, insufficient inventory can result in stock shortages, delayed order fulfillment, lost sales, and reduced customer satisfaction.
Traditional inventory management practices often rely on historical averages and manual estimates. Although these approaches can be useful, they may not adequately account for seasonal changes, purchasing patterns, and fluctuations in customer demand.
Data science provides opportunities to improve inventory planning through statistical analysis, predictive modeling, and optimization techniques.
By examining historical transaction data, businesses can identify demand patterns, estimate future product requirements, and make better-informed purchasing decisions.
2.2 Logistics Scenario
The selected scenario involves a medium-sized retail distribution company that supplies household products, gifts, and consumer goods through an online retail platform.
The company operates a central distribution warehouse and manages hundreds of different products.
The organization experiences several operational challenges:
1. Unpredictable customer demand
Customer demand changes over time, making it difficult to estimate the quantities of products required.
2. Excess inventory
Products with low demand may occupy valuable warehouse space and increase inventory holding costs.
3. Stock shortages
Popular products may become unavailable because purchasing decisions are not based on reliable demand estimates.
4. Inefficient purchasing decisions
Procurement teams may lack accurate forecasting information when determining order quantities.
5. Poor resource allocation
Warehouse space and financial resources may not be distributed according to product demand and sales performance.
To address these challenges, the company proposes introducing a data-driven inventory planning framework.
The proposed framework will analyze historical sales transactions, identify product demand patterns, estimate future demand, and support inventory replenishment decisions.
2.3 Problem Statement
The organization currently lacks a reliable analytical framework for predicting product demand and determining suitable inventory levels.
This limitation may contribute to excessive inventory costs, product shortages, inefficient warehouse utilization, and poor purchasing decisions.
The project therefore investigates how Python-based data analytics and machine learning techniques can support improved demand forecasting and inventory planning.
