4. KEY PERFORMANCE INDICATORS (KPIs)
Key Performance Indicators are measurable values used to evaluate the efficiency and effectiveness of business operations.
The project will consider five major KPIs.
4.1 Demand Forecast Accuracy
Demand forecast accuracy evaluates how closely predicted demand matches actual demand.
The project will use Weighted Absolute Percentage Error (WAPE).
Formula:
WAPE = (Sum of Absolute Forecast Errors / Sum of Actual Demand) × 100
A lower WAPE indicates better forecasting performance.
Business relevance: Helps procurement teams evaluate the reliability of demand predictions.
4.2 Inventory Turnover Ratio
Inventory turnover measures how frequently inventory is sold and replaced during a specified period.
Formula:
Inventory Turnover = Cost of Goods Sold / Average Inventory Value
A higher turnover ratio can indicate efficient inventory utilization, although excessively high turnover may also indicate insufficient inventory.
Business relevance: Helps management identify fast-moving and slow-moving products.
4.3 Stockout Rate
Stockout rate measures the proportion of product-demand opportunities that cannot be fulfilled because the required products are unavailable.
Formula:
Stockout Rate = (Number of Stockout Events / Total Product Availability Opportunities) × 100
A lower stockout rate indicates improved product availability.
Business relevance: Helps identify products requiring improved replenishment planning.
4.4 Order Fulfillment Rate
Order fulfillment rate measures the percentage of customer orders successfully fulfilled.
Formula:
Order Fulfillment Rate = (Orders Fulfilled Completely / Total Orders) × 100
A higher fulfillment rate indicates better logistics service performance.
Business relevance: Evaluates whether improved inventory planning supports better customer service.
4.5 Inventory Holding Cost
Inventory holding cost represents the expense associated with storing products over a specified period.
Formula:
Inventory Holding Cost = Average Inventory Value × Holding Cost Rate
Holding costs may include warehouse rent, insurance, handling expenses, and capital costs.
Business relevance: Helps management understand the financial impact of excess inventory.
4.6 KPI Summary
KPI	Measurement	Desired Direction
Forecast accuracy (WAPE)	Forecasting error	Lower
Inventory turnover	Inventory utilization	Appropriate improvement
Stockout rate	Product unavailability	Lower
Order fulfillment rate	Orders completed	Higher
Inventory holding cost	Storage expenses	Lower
These indicators provide a balanced approach to evaluating forecasting performance, inventory efficiency, and customer service.
