5. APPLICATION OF DATA SCIENCE TECHNIQUES
The proposed project will apply several data science methodologies to investigate inventory management challenges.
5.1 Exploratory Data Analysis
Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) involves examining datasets to understand their structure, identify patterns, and detect data quality problems.
The proposed analysis includes:
•	Identifying missing values.
•	Examining duplicate transactions.
•	Analyzing product quantities.
•	Identifying frequently purchased products.
•	Examining monthly and daily sales trends.
•	Investigating seasonal purchasing patterns.
•	Visualizing product demand distributions.
The Python libraries Pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib, and Seaborn will support this process.
5.2 Regression Analysis
Regression analysis is used to estimate relationships between variables and predict numerical outcomes.
In this project, regression models will be investigated to estimate future product demand.
Potential input variables include:
•	Historical sales quantities.
•	Previous-day demand.
•	Previous-week demand.
•	Previous-month demand.
•	Month of the year.
•	Day of the week.
•	Seasonal indicators.
Potential models include:
•	Linear Regression.
•	Random Forest Regression.
•	Gradient Boosting Regression.
The models will be compared using appropriate forecasting evaluation metrics.
5.3 Time-Series Forecasting
Time-series forecasting uses historical observations recorded over time to estimate future values.
Retail demand can change because of seasonal effects, customer preferences, promotions, and market conditions.
The project will investigate:
•	Moving average forecasting.
•	Exponential smoothing.
•	Seasonal forecasting methods.
•	Regression-based forecasting.
These techniques can support estimates of future product requirements.
5.4 Clustering Analysis
Clustering is an unsupervised machine learning technique used to group similar observations.
In inventory management, products can be categorized according to their sales and demand characteristics.
The proposed K-Means algorithm will investigate three broad product segments:
Segment	Characteristics	Possible Management Action
High demand	Strong sales activity	Monitor availability closely
Medium demand	Moderate sales activity	Maintain balanced inventory
Low demand	Relatively weak sales activity	Review replenishment quantities
Clustering variables may include average demand, total demand, transaction frequency, revenue, and demand variability.
This approach can help management prioritize warehouse space and purchasing resources.
5.5 Inventory Optimization
Optimization techniques help identify suitable inventory decisions while considering business constraints.
Forecasted demand can be combined with actual inventory records, supplier lead times, and replenishment costs.
Two important inventory concepts will be investigated.
Reorder Point (ROP)
ROP = Average Daily Demand × Supplier Lead Time + Safety Stock
Economic Order Quantity (EOQ)
EOQ = Square Root of [(2 × Annual Demand × Ordering Cost) / Annual Holding Cost per Unit]
These formulas provide a foundation for inventory planning.
More advanced optimization techniques may be introduced when multiple warehouses, suppliers, purchasing budgets, and storage constraints are considered.
