6. STRATEGIC ROADMAP AND METHODOLOGY
The proposed project will follow a structured analytical process from data collection to management recommendations.
6.1 Stage 1: Data Collection
Historical retail transaction data will be collected from the UCI Machine Learning Repository.
The dataset contains:
•	Invoice numbers.
•	Product codes.
•	Product descriptions.
•	Purchased quantities.
•	Transaction dates.
•	Unit prices.
•	Customer identifiers.
•	Country information.
Additional operational information should be collected where available, including inventory records, purchase orders, supplier lead times, and warehouse expenses.
Expected output: A consolidated dataset suitable for analysis.
6.2 Stage 2: Data Cleaning and Preparation
Raw datasets may contain missing values, duplicate records, incorrect formats, and unusual observations.
The following activities will be performed:
1.	Load the dataset using Pandas.
2.	Examine column names and data types.
3.	Identify missing values.
4.	Remove duplicate transactions where appropriate.
5.	Convert transaction dates into datetime format.
6.	Identify cancellations and returns.
7.	Validate product quantities and prices.
8.	Prepare a consistent product-level demand dataset.
Returns and cancellations will be handled separately to avoid incorrectly reducing estimates of gross demand.
Expected output: A cleaned and structured dataset.
6.3 Stage 3: Exploratory Data Analysis
The cleaned dataset will be examined to identify important patterns.
The analysis will investigate:
•	Total sales by product.
•	Average daily and weekly demand.
•	Monthly sales trends.
•	Top-performing products.
•	Low-demand products.
•	Demand variability.
•	Seasonal purchasing patterns.
Visualizations will include bar charts, line graphs, histograms, and correlation heatmaps.
Expected output: An understanding of product demand and retail sales behavior.
6.4 Stage 4: Feature Engineering
Feature engineering involves creating additional variables that improve the predictive capabilities of machine learning models.
The proposed features include:
•	Previous-day demand.
•	Previous-week demand.
•	Previous-month demand.
•	Seven-day moving average.
•	Thirty-day moving average.
•	Month.
•	Week number.
•	Day of the week.
•	Seasonal indicators.
Expected output: A structured feature dataset for predictive modeling.
6.5 Stage 5: Predictive Modeling
The next stage involves developing and training forecasting models.
The proposed models include:
1.	Moving Average Forecasting.
2.	Linear Regression.
3.	Random Forest Regression.
4.	Exponential Smoothing.
The models will be trained using historical observations and tested against later observations.
A chronological train-test split or time-series cross-validation will be used to prevent future information from entering the training process.
Expected output: Forecasted product demand for a future planning period.
6.6 Stage 6: Product Segmentation
K-Means clustering will be applied to categorize products according to their demand characteristics.
The analysis will investigate high-demand, medium-demand, and low-demand product groups.
The results can help the warehouse management team determine which products require greater attention and more accessible storage locations.
Expected output: Product demand segments.
6.7 Stage 7: Inventory Planning and Optimization
Forecasting results will be combined with actual inventory information and supplier records.
The organization will estimate:
•	Reorder points.
•	Safety stock requirements.
•	Economic order quantities.
•	Potential excess inventory.
•	Products at risk of stockouts.
Expected output: Data-supported inventory replenishment recommendations.
6.8 Stage 8: Performance Evaluation and Reporting
The final stage involves evaluating forecasting performance and translating analytical results into practical recommendations.
Forecasting performance will be measured using:
•	Mean Absolute Error (MAE).
•	Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).
•	Weighted Absolute Percentage Error (WAPE).
Operational KPIs will be calculated where the required inventory and fulfillment data are available.
Expected output: A strategic logistics analytics report containing visualizations and management recommendations.
6.9 End-to-End Analytical Process
Stage	Activity	Main Tool	Deliverable
1	Data collection	UCI Repository	Raw dataset
