7. PYTHON IMPLEMENTATION AND CODE ILLUSTRATIONS
The project will use Python to implement the proposed analytical methods.
The following code examples illustrate the main stages of the implementation.
7.1 Importing Required Libraries
python
import pandas as pd
import numpy as np

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import seaborn as sns

from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor
from sklearn.cluster import KMeans
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler

from sklearn.metrics import (
    mean_absolute_error,
    mean_squared_error
)
7.2 Loading the Dataset
python
df = pd.read_excel(
    "data/online_retail_II.xlsx"
)

print(df.head())

print(df.info())

print(df.describe())
7.3 Data Cleaning
python
df = df.drop_duplicates()

df["InvoiceDate"] = pd.to_datetime(
    df["InvoiceDate"],
    errors="coerce"
)

df["InvoiceNo"] = df["InvoiceNo"].astype(str)

cancelled = df["InvoiceNo"].str.startswith("C")

sales = df[
    (~cancelled) &
    (df["Quantity"] > 0) &
    (df["Price"] > 0)
].dropna(
    subset=["StockCode", "InvoiceDate"]
).copy()

sales["Revenue"] = (
    sales["Quantity"] * sales["Price"]
)

print("Data cleaning completed!")
7.4 Exploratory Data Analysis
python
product_sales = (
    sales.groupby("StockCode")["Quantity"]
    .sum()
    .sort_values(ascending=False)
)

product_sales.head(10).plot(
    kind="bar",
    figsize=(10, 5)
)

plt.title("Top 10 Best-Selling Products")
plt.xlabel("Product Code")
plt.ylabel("Quantity Sold")

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()
7.5 Monthly Sales Analysis
python
sales["Revenue"] = (
    sales["Quantity"] * sales["Price"]
)

monthly_sales = (
    sales.set_index("InvoiceDate")
    .resample("MS")["Revenue"]
    .sum()
)

monthly_sales.plot(
    figsize=(12, 5),
    color="green"
)

plt.title("Monthly Retail Revenue")
plt.xlabel("Month")
plt.ylabel("Revenue")

plt.show()
7.6 Demand Forecasting
python
daily_demand = (
    sales.groupby(
        sales["InvoiceDate"].dt.floor("D")
    )["Quantity"]
    .sum()
    .asfreq("D", fill_value=0)
)

forecast_data = pd.DataFrame({
    "demand": daily_demand
})

forecast_data["lag_1"] = (
    forecast_data["demand"].shift(1)
)

forecast_data["lag_7"] = (
    forecast_data["demand"].shift(7)
)

forecast_data["rolling_mean"] = (
    forecast_data["demand"]
    .shift(1)
    .rolling(7)
    .mean()
)

forecast_data = forecast_data.dropna()

X = forecast_data[
    ["lag_1", "lag_7", "rolling_mean"]
]

y = forecast_data["demand"]

split = int(len(X) * 0.8)

X_train = X.iloc[:split]
X_test = X.iloc[split:]

y_train = y.iloc[:split]
y_test = y.iloc[split:]

model = RandomForestRegressor(
    n_estimators=100,
    random_state=42
)

model.fit(X_train, y_train)

predictions = model.predict(X_test)

mae = mean_absolute_error(
    y_test, predictions
)

rmse = np.sqrt(
    mean_squared_error(y_test, predictions)
)

print("MAE:", mae)
print("RMSE:", rmse)
The example demonstrates daily demand forecasting across the aggregated dataset. Product-specific forecasting will be performed separately when required.
7.7 Product Clustering
python
product_features = sales.groupby(
    "StockCode"
).agg(
    total_demand=("Quantity", "sum"),
    average_demand=("Quantity", "mean"),
    total_revenue=("Revenue", "sum")
).fillna(0)

scaled = StandardScaler().fit_transform(
    np.log1p(product_features)
)

kmeans = KMeans(
    n_clusters=3,
    random_state=42,
    n_init=10
)

product_features["Cluster"] = (
    kmeans.fit_predict(scaled)
)

print(product_features.head())
7.8 Reorder Point Calculation
python
average_daily_demand = 50

supplier_lead_time = 7

safety_stock = 100

reorder_point = (
    average_daily_demand * supplier_lead_time
    + safety_stock
)

print("Reorder Point:", reorder_point)
The illustrative calculation produces a reorder point of 450 units.
The example uses hypothetical values and is not an actual result from the public retail dataset.
