10. PROJECT RISKS AND LIMITATIONS
Although the proposed analytical approach provides several opportunities, certain risks and limitations must be considered.
10.1 Missing Data
The public retail dataset contains missing values.
Incorrect handling of missing records may affect the quality of the analysis.
Mitigation: Perform systematic data quality checks and document data cleaning decisions.
10.2 Historical Data Limitations
Historical purchasing behavior may not always represent future demand.
Mitigation: Compare different forecasting models and periodically update predictions.
10.3 Limited Inventory Information
The selected dataset does not contain actual warehouse stock balances, supplier lead times, or stockout events.
Mitigation: Obtain additional operational information or use clearly labeled illustrative values during initial testing.
10.4 Seasonal Variations
Promotions, holidays, and unexpected changes in customer preferences may influence demand.
Mitigation: Incorporate calendar information and promotional variables where available.
10.5 Model Accuracy
No forecasting model can guarantee perfect predictions.
Mitigation: Use time-based validation, compare against baseline forecasting methods, and regularly monitor model performance.
10.6 Data Assumptions
Days without recorded transactions may be interpreted as zero demand. However, this assumption must be checked against business operating days and dataset completeness.
Mitigation: Validate the demand calendar before using zero-filled dates for forecasting.
