12. CONCLUSION
This strategic planning project demonstrates how data science can support more efficient logistics and inventory management.
The selected retail distribution scenario highlights common operational challenges, including inaccurate demand forecasting, excess inventory, stock shortages, and inefficient resource allocation.
By using publicly available retail transaction data and Python-based analytical tools, the proposed project will investigate how historical purchasing patterns can be transformed into meaningful business insights.
Exploratory Data Analysis will help identify sales patterns and product behavior. Regression and time-series forecasting will estimate future demand, while clustering will categorize products according to their sales characteristics.
Inventory optimization techniques will then help translate these findings into practical replenishment recommendations.
The proposed KPIs provide a measurable framework for evaluating forecasting accuracy, inventory utilization, product availability, fulfillment performance, and holding costs.
Overall, the project establishes a foundation for applying data-driven decision-making to logistics operations. It demonstrates how predictive analytics can support better purchasing decisions, reduce avoidable inventory expenses, improve warehouse resource allocation, and strengthen supply chain efficiency.
The long-term recommendation is for logistics organizations to integrate predictive analytics into their inventory management processes and continuously evaluate operational performance using reliable data.
